# DESIGN MEMO
# ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# I used lists, sets, loops, and set intersections to build
# the recommendation tool. A list is useful for storing the
# customer's preferences while they are being entered because
# values can be added as the program runs. I then converted
# the preferences to a set so duplicate preferences are
# automatically removed. I also converted each product's tags
# into a set. This allows the program to use set intersection
# to quickly find tags that appear in both the product and the
# customer's preferences. The count of the intersection gives
# the product's match score. I used loops to go through the
# products and calculate a score for each one. Products with
# at least one matching tag are added to the recommendation
# list, and the final list is sorted from the highest score to
# the lowest score so the most relevant products appear first.
#
# If the catalog grew to 1,000 or more products, this basic
# approach could still work, but the program would need to be
# optimized for a larger system. Converting tags to sets is
# already helpful because set membership and intersection
# operations are efficient. For a much larger catalog, I could
# create an index that maps each tag to the products containing
# that tag. Then, instead of checking every product for every
# customer, the program could quickly find products associated
# with the customer's preferred tags. A database could also
# store the product catalog instead of keeping everything in
# memory. Caching frequently requested recommendations could
# further improve performance. These changes would make the
# system more scalable while keeping the same basic
# recommendation logic.
